1. Successful signals: Strongest negative scores (reductions in IFNG) overwhelmingly pinpoint T-cell receptor (TCR) signaling, proximal costimulation, and associated kinases/adaptors (e.g., ZAP70, VAV1, LCP2, PLCG1, CD3 subunits, LCK, ITK, TRADD, MALT1). Strong positive scores (enhancements in IFNG) highlight negative regulators of transcription and translation/metabolism, such as CBLB, NFKB2, RNF20, and TSC1.

2. Weak signals: Scores clustering near zero or showing minimal deviation involve generic metabolic enzymes, structural proteins, and peripheral cytokine receptors not directly wired into core T-cell activation or proximal IFNG transcriptional control pathways.

3. Working hypothesis: Interferon-gamma production in this context is heavily dependent on core T-cell receptor signaling cascades and downstream transcription factor networks. Productive signal originates from disrupting positive proximal activation complexes (which drastically reduce IFNG) or relieving negative feedback checkpoints and transcriptional repressors (which enhance IFNG).

4. Next-round principle: Prioritize exploitation around core TCR signalosomes, proximal phosphorylation networks, and established negative feedback loops. Maintain a minimal exploration budget to probe adjacent nodes in immune cell activation pathways to capture unexplored regulatory hubs.
